# Flow API design

General Facts:

There are two kinds of errors in Fearless: deterministic and non deterministic, like stack overflow. `Try#` catches deterministic errors anywhere; observing a non-deterministic one needs `mut CapTry` from `mut System`.
In the current standard library Repr is mostly complete, but IsoPod is still under design and it may end up offering most of the same functionalities of Repr; thus allowing mutation and reads of the IsoPod without opening it.

## Properties to be respected by any part of the language

**Determinism:**\
A method with no mut parameters (including the receiver) returns structurally identical results for structurally identical input. The term "results" extends to deterministic exceptions and termination: the structurally identical result may mean non termination or a structurally identical thrown Info.

One of the core ways this works is that all methods of object capabilities actually doing something non deterministic are mut methods, thus with no mut in input, any capability in the ROG of the operation is 'muted'. Most of the properties below also enable determinism, but they are also valuable on their own.


**Immutability:**\
An imm reference refers to an object whose ROG is fully deeply unmodifiable. We can assume that nothing in that ROG will change ever again.

**read:**\
A read reference can not be used to mutate an object or anything in its ROG.
Note that if a method has no mut parameters, any read parameter is also deeply unmodifiable for the duration of that method call, since there is no way a mut reference to the ROG of that read reference could be in scope. This allows us to reason inductively about deterministic parallelism: if a read binding `r` is in scope, there will be no other active parallel execution with a mut reference to any part of `r`'s ROG.

**iso:**\
An iso reference refers to an object with (mutable) ROG encapsulated under that single reference. imm objects can be shared freely since they are referentially transparent, but all mutable objects under that ROG would be garbage collectable if that iso reference were lost.

**Strong exception safety:**\
The body of a try either completes successfully or leaves the state observable outside of the try unmodified. Enforced by typing: `Try#[R](try: read F[R])`. A `read` lambda cannot mutate what it captures, so the body reaches only what it created or the `iso` given to the two-argument form, and `Action` yields the value or the `Info`.

Example:
For
`Try#{.. rs.flow(ParRepr).map{r -> r.mutate{...}}.list}`
to compile, with `rs` of type `mut List[mut Repr[T]]`,
`rs` can not be captured by the `Try` lambda.
It must be locally created inside the `{..` part.
Thus running that flow as a fork join is correct: even if element 3 of 7 of `rs` were to throw, we would not be able to observe non determinism since all of ROG(rs) is garbage collectable at the point the error is captured.


**Identity Transparency:**\
Without the mut methods of capability objects, we can not distinguish whether two references are the same object in memory/object identity or just structurally equivalent.\
This needs to be enforced by careful API design in all the `_Magic!` set ups. This is needed for Determinism and caching: without this one could call something twice and discover re-computation vs access to cached result, in turn allowing one to violate Determinism of an outer expression internally using caching (and testing for identity).

**Flow unobservable parallelism:**\
It must not be observable if a potentially parallel flow actually runs in parallel or sequentially. This is (intentionally) slightly stronger than what is strictly needed to simply not violate determinism.

Note that where a method contract is purposely under-specified, such as the tournament order in max, min and sort, the same choice must be made in every mode. A different winner under parallel execution than under sequential execution makes the mode observable, violating Flow unobservable parallelism.


**Flow abstraction:**\
Observably, the flow must behave as if it trickled the elements through the various stages one at a time.
As an exception, a fence operation emits nothing downstream until every stage before it has finished with every element, and a fence operation whose lambda can mutate what it captured (today only .forEach) does not run that lambda until then either. A fence operation may still work as elements arrive - a sort may place each one as it is received - provided nothing escapes before the stages before it have settled, a thrown Info included.

It must not be observable if a potentially parallel flow actually runs the stages one at a time or passes the elements one at a time through all the stages, if it does internal materializations or not etc.


**Flows overall design:**\
A flow expression is a source, then stages, then one terminal.

**Lazy:** Until the terminal is triggered, no user provided lambdas in any of the flow methods should be run.
The semantics should be defined by multiple descriptions, and validated by checking that all those descriptions provide the same semantic.

Semantic 1: the simple composition of all the stages and the terminal.

Semantic 2: by desugaring all flow operations into other more primitive ones, until a few operations remain that can be given an un-optimised but trivially correct direct implementation.

Semantic 3: by examples of many thorny cases.

Semantic 4: by translation of full flow expressions into traditional imperative statements.

Execution strategies are defined by the terminal operation looking at all the stages in the chain. This can be used for optimizations, like avoiding internal materialization steps when needed.


**Stages** are the intermediate operations returning another flow.

**Elementwise:** `map`, `filter`, `flatMap`, `flatMapMut`. The output for an element depends only on that element, so any element can run on any thread.

**Streaming stateful:** `limit`, `scan`, `actor`, `actorMut`, `map with context`, `distinct`. Needs the elements in order and emits as it goes.

**Fence:** `sort`, `sortDistinct`, `max`, `min`. Consumes everything before emitting anything.

**Source combinator:** `++`. Appends the elements of a list to those of the flow, rather than acting on elements.

**Terminals** end the flow and return something that is not a flow.

**Consuming:** `list`, `eList`, `set`, `eSet`, `mapping`, `forEach`, `fold`, `foldAcc`, `size`, `get`, `getOpt`, `join`, `sum`, `last`, `let`. Every element passes the whole chain.

**Short-circuit:** `first`, `any`, `all`, `none`, `findFirst`, `findMap`. Can decide before the end, leaving later elements unreached.

**Execution modes:**\
Using the static type of the initial collection we can enable one or more execution modes. They are a crystallization of what we know and what we can assume; they are used to allow/block various execution strategies for the terminal, including parallelization and omission of intermediate materializations.

Note that they are not about the type of the elements in the middle of the stages. A stage of a flow in execution mode `Par` may create mutable objects, and another stage may mutate them: over an `imm List[imm Nat]`, `l.flow.map{i -> Persons#("Bob", i)}.map{p -> ...}` runs in parallel and every (mut) `Person` it builds is reachable from nowhere else, so later stages can mutate it safely.

**Duplicated `mut` elements:**\
`flatMapMut` can emit one `mut` object as two elements, and so can `actorMut`, by pushing its element twice:
```
numbers.flow.map{n -> Persons#n}.flatMapMut{p -> Lists#(p, p)}.filter{..}.list
```
Each `Person` now appears twice, so two tasks could run `filter` on the same `Person` at the same time: the lambda of `filter` is a `read F`, which can not mutate what it captured but can mutate its `mut` parameter. Two elements can also share a `Person` without being it, like two pairs holding the same `Person`, so no identity check can find this.
So the note above stops holding after a `flatMapMut` or an `actorMut`: the elements they produce from the same input may share `mut` objects, and can not be handed to different tasks. Any strategy that hands the elements after them to different tasks, like splitting again after a fence or running the stages after an `actorMut` in parallel, has to respect this.

**`Par[T:imm,read]`:** can be enabled if source elements are `imm` or `read`.

**`ParRepr[T]`:** can be enabled if source elements are `mut Repr[T]`. `Reprs#` takes an `iso T`, so two distinct `Repr`s share no inner state. Note that this does not ensure that those `Repr` objects have different identity.

**`ParPod[T]`:** can be enabled if source elements are `mut IsoPod[T]`, with the same guarantee through `IsoPods#`. Note that this does not ensure that those `IsoPod` objects have different identity.

**`onlyImmCapture` flag:**\
The backend marks each lambda object that captures only `imm` values; the flag is visible to the runtime (in Java), not in Fearless.
`ParRepr` and `ParPod` need it on every lambda of the flow except those of `forEach` and `let`, which run once every stage has finished; without it the flow raises a non deterministic error (see below).
The identity check only compares the elements with each other, while a `read` lambda can capture any `mut` binding as `read`: the source list itself, or a local or parameter `p: mut Repr[T]` that is also one of the elements. Such a lambda can read that `Repr` while the task of its element mutates it, and the parallel execution becomes observable.
No `imm` value can reach a `mut Repr` or `mut IsoPod`, so with the flag each task reaches no `Repr` or `IsoPod` but its own element.
