`.seqFlow` exists only on sources whose elements may be `mut`, such as `List` and `EList`, to flow them as `mut` without a mode. Where the elements are always `read` or `imm`, as in `Set` and `ESet`, `.flow` is the only entry point: running in parallel or sequentially is not observable there, so a preference for sequential execution is only a hint (see **Chunk sizes**).

Calling a `.flow` with a set of calls suggesting that parallelism is possible, while passing parameters or calling methods that would prevent any parallelism, is most likely a user mistake. When the type system can not check this statically, a non deterministic dynamic error is raised. A linter could try to catch more cases statically, but could never do the full job since Flows under construction are first class entities that can freely navigate in the source.

