### ++

`mut ++ (b: read List[E]): mut Flow[read/imm E]`

With parallelisation in mind this is a useful operator both in the par mode and to start parallelisation after the fact.
Take a flow that may or may not be parallel, going from some source into `Flow[E]`, and append all the elements of the list `b`.
After the `++` every element is `read` or `imm`, so no stage after it can mutate anything that existed before the flow: even if the receiver was not a parallel flow, it is now, and the stages after the `++` can run in data parallel on all the elements, the ones of the receiver and the ones of `b`.
They can also overlap with the stages before the `++`, in pipeline, unless those stages mutate existing objects (a sequential, `ParRepr` or `ParPod` source) and the elements are `read`: an element after the `++` could then reach an object those stages are still mutating for a later element, so the stages after the `++` start once the stages before it have finished, and the `++` works as a fence.
This makes it possible to start parallel execution after some sequential stage, like
`persons.seqFlow.map{p->p.computeDNA}++{}.map{..}.list`
This is arguably not the most readable code, but by pushing the empty list in, we get type safe knowledge that the second map can be data-parallel.



### size, get, getOpt

Size is just a sugar of `.list.size`.
We chose to align the behaviour with `.list.size` even if in some cases we could be faster, because
the possible optimizations are really brittle;
On many other systems we would have
```
es.map.size//fast
es.map.filter.size//slow
```

`get` and `getOpt` are sugar over `.list` in the same way:
```
.get -> this.list |> {xs -> xs.size == 1 ? {
  .then -> xs.getFirst;
  .else -> Error.msg(`Flow.get: the flow has `+(xs.size)+` elements, but .get needs exactly one`)}};
.getOpt -> this.list |> {xs -> xs.size <= 1 ? {
  .then -> xs.first;
  .else -> Error.msg(`Flow.getOpt: the flow has `+(xs.size)+` elements, but .getOpt needs at most one`)}};
```
The size is checked once every element has passed the whole chain, so
```
Lists#(1,2,3).flow.map{x -> x == 3 ? {.then -> Error.msg`three`; .else -> x}}.get
```
raises `three`, not the error about the size.
Deciding at the second element would make them short-circuit terminals, and every element computed past the second would be positive speculation, which is not safe over `ParRepr` and `ParPod`.
As consuming terminals they are parallelised exactly like `.list`.

### mapping

`l.flow.map f .mapping(by, kem)`

Let `put` be what the terminal does with one element: `put#(x)` applies `kem.key` and `kem.elem`
to `x`, uses `by` to place the key among the keys already present, and throws when the key is
already present. Which calls `put` makes is left under-specified, as under `.sort`.
If there is a smallest index `j` such that `put#(f#ej)` throws or does not terminate,
the whole flow expression throws that same `Info` or does not terminate.

Otherwise this flow returns the map holding one entry per element, in index order.

Two elements carrying the same key make `put` throw at the later of the two indices, so the flow
raises rather than keeping one value and dropping the other.

In other flow/stream libraries the same chain may keep either value without a word.

