**example with let**\
```
l.flow
  .map f
  .let[Nat, Void] total = {g -> g.sum 0}
  .map{n -> n + total}
  .list
```

If a call of `f` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression throws the same `Info` or does not terminate.\
Otherwise this flow returns the list holding `f#ej + total` at each index `j`, where `total` is the sum of `f#ej` over every index `j`.

The result of every `f#ej` is needed twice, so `let` materialises the flow up to that point as a list.

**example with forEach**\
`l.flow.map{_ -> v.get} .forEach{x -> Block#(acc.add x, v.set(v.get + 1))}`\
with `v` a fresh `mut Var[Nat]` holding `0`, and `acc` a fresh `mut EList[Nat]`.

If there is a smallest index `j` such that `v.get |> {x -> Block#(acc.add x, v.set(v.get + 1))}` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression throws that same `Info` or does not terminate.\
Otherwise this flow returns `Void`, and afterwards `acc` holds eight times `0` and `v` holds `8`.

The `map` stage lambda reads `v` and the terminal lambda writes `v`. Crucially `.forEach` is a fence operation.

In many other flow/streams approaches this may behave very differently; the forEach is not a fence and we may end up with `acc` holding `0` to `7` instead. The fence is required by Fearless flows to allow for parallelism. `.forEach` requires, by its own nature, to be able to do side effects on mut captured parameters.

With the fence behaviour the stage can be parallelised: every `v.get` reads the same `0`, and no stage call reads what the terminal writes, because the terminal is fenced later and the terminal forEach is the only terminal that can mutate existing data.

**example with filter, over ParRepr**\
```
rs.flow(ParRepr)
  .filter{r -> r.mutate{
    c -> Block#(c++, c.get)}
    > 3}
  .size
```
If any two `Repr`s (or `IsoPod`s in an equivalent example) are identity equivalent, a non deterministic exception is thrown.

If there is a smallest index `j` such that `rj.mutate{c -> Block#(c++, c.get)}` throws or does not terminate, the whole flow expression throws that same `Info` or does not terminate. Thanks to strong exception safety we will never be able to observe how many elements in the flow/list have been mutated.\
Otherwise this flow returns the number of indices `j` where `rj` holds more than `3` after being increased, and afterwards every `rj` in `rs` holds one more than `rj` held before the flow.

Every element is increased, the rejected elements included, and `rs` still holds every element, so the increases are readable once the flow has returned.

The filter can be parallelised while `size` is the terminal.

Consider the same example but with a short-circuit terminal like `.any` in place of `size`
```
rs.flow(ParRepr)
  .filter{r -> r.mutate{
    c -> Block#(c++, c.get)}
    > 3}
  .any {r->r.look{..}}
```
In many other approaches the parallelisation becomes (positive) speculative, and the increases on elements past the decision survive the flow.\
We can not accept this.
This particular code is exactly the case of code screaming
'parallel' that is only safe to execute sequentially.
This code would throw a non deterministic exception, even when the optimizer would 'want' to run it sequentially.

The only sound option is to somehow insert a fence before
any operation that would need (positive) speculative execution to be properly optimized in parallel, as shown below:
```
rs.flow(ParRepr)
  .filter{r -> r.mutate{
    c -> Block#(c++, c.get)}
    > 3}
  .sort {..}
  .any {r->r.look{..}}
```
Overall parallelizing `Repr`s and `IsoPod`s is about
- running an identity check
- checking the `onlyImmCapture` flag on the lambdas of the flow
- avoiding unfenced (positive) speculative execution and running fenced (positive) speculative execution sequentially
- behaving exactly as if a par flow produced a mut out of the first mapping: those muts are guaranteed to be isolated with respect to each other and to what the lambdas captured, thus they can not interact, until a `flatMapMut` or an `actorMut` duplicates them (see **Duplicated `mut` elements**). Depending on the stages, data parallel or nodes could be more appropriate.

**example with `++`, over pods**\
```
ps1.flow(ParPod).map{p -> p.mutate{..}}
  ++ (ps2.flow(ParPod).map{p -> p.mutate{..}}.list)
  .list
```
with `ps1` and `ps2` of type `mut List[mut IsoPod[T]]`.

The argument of `++` is an ordinary expression, evaluated when `++` is called: the flow over `ps2` runs to its `.list`, starting with the identity check on the pods of `ps2`, while the flow over `ps1` is only described and none of its pods is looked at yet.
Then the final `.list` runs the flow over `ps1`: the identity check on the pods of `ps1` and the `onlyImmCapture` flag on its lambdas come first, and a pod found twice raises a non deterministic error.
If there is a smallest index `j` such that the `map` over `ps1` throws or does not terminate on the `j`-th pod, the whole flow expression throws that same `Info` or does not terminate. Otherwise it returns the results over `ps1`, followed by the results over `ps2`.

The two flows never run at the same time, so a pod in both lists is not an error: the `map` over `ps2` mutates it first, then the `map` over `ps1`. For the same reason the argument could also read pods of `ps1`, for example through `read Box` elements holding them: those reads happen before any pod of `ps1` is mutated.

To handle the pods of both lists as one parallel source, concatenate the lists before flowing them:
```
(ps1 ++ ps2).flow(ParPod).map{p -> p.mutate{..}}.list
```
The identity check of `.list` then covers the union: a pod found twice, in the same list or once in each, raises a non deterministic error, and once it passes the `map` can run on all the pods in parallel.


